 @AuthenticationPrincipal is
 Spring Security's way of handing your controller the already-verified logged-in user
 — as a typed object, straight into a method parameter.
                                                                                                                                                                                     
  The journey of a request:                                                                                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                                                                     
  client sends "Authorization: Bearer <JWT>"                                                                                                                                         
          ↓                                                                                                                                                                          
  JwtAuthenticationFilter  →  parses + validates the JWT, builds an AuthenticatedUser                                                                                                
          ↓                         (accountId, telegramUserId, role, displayName)                                                                                                   
  SecurityContext  ←  stores it for the duration of the request                                                                                                                      
          ↓                                                                                                                                                                          
  controller method receives it via @AuthenticationPrincipal AuthenticatedUser owner



  So the annotation is just Spring saying:
  "take the principal out of the SecurityContext and inject it here."
  Our principal is your AuthenticatedUser record
  — that's why every owner endpoint can write owner.accountId() and be sure it's the caller's real ID.

  Why it matters: identity comes from the verified token, never from the request body.
                  Without this pattern, a controller might take an ownerId from JSON — and then any client could
                  pretend to be any owner.
                   With @AuthenticationPrincipal, the client only controls the token;
                   the filter controls who the token says they are.

  The manual equivalent would be:

  AuthenticatedUser owner = (AuthenticatedUser) SecurityContextHolder.getContext()
          .getAuthentication().getPrincipal();

  — verbose and repeated everywhere; the annotation does that for free.